<article class="env-lemma" id="0BH2">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BH2">Lemma <span data-tag="0BH2">72.4.4</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an integral algebraic space over $S$. Then $\Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ is a domain. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Set $R = \Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$. If $f, g \in R$ are nonzero and $fg = 0$ then $X = V(f) \cup V(g)$ where $V(f)$ denotes the closed subspace of $X$ cut out by $f$. Since $X$ is irreducible, we see that either $V(f) = X$ or $V(g) = X$. Then either $f = 0$ or $g = 0$ by Properties of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/0BGS" data-tag="0BGS">66.21.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>